$$ Commands for catch_failfast.ps1. Kept in a file rather than passed with -c
$$ because the command string has to contain quotes, and a quoted argument
$$ containing quotes does not survive PowerShell handing it to cdb - the outer
$$ quotes are stripped and cdb reads the remainder as a program to launch. A
$$ script file has no shell in the path, so cdb's own parser sees it intact.

.symfix

$$ Unity publishes symbols for shipped players, and this build's are already
$$ downloaded and expanded under symbols\. Without them a fail-fast stack is a
$$ column of UnityPlayer+hex, which is how five offsets sat in the logs for days
$$ while the crash they named - GfxDeviceD3D11Base::PresentFrame walking a
$$ std::list<ID3D11Query *> - went unrecognised. The local path is first so a
$$ cached copy is used before the network is touched.
.sympath+ F:\rbo_fabre_proto\d3d9_sw\symbols
.sympath+ SRV*F:\rbo_fabre_proto\d3d9_sw\symbols-cache*https://symbolserver.unity3d.com/
.reload /f
.echo === symbol state for the modules that matter ===
lm m UnityPlayer
lm m mono*
lm m d3d11

$$ Fail-fast. Windows reports every one of these as 0xc0000409,
$$ STATUS_STACK_BUFFER_OVERRUN, whatever the real check was; the subcode in rcx
$$ is what names it, and 3 is FAST_FAIL_CORRUPT_LIST_ENTRY. kv is the point of
$$ the whole exercise: the call site tells us which list ntdll was walking.
$$ A full dump is written too. The stack in this log names the site, but the dump
$$ is the only way to ask a question we did not think of before the process died
$$ - which has happened twice now - and it can be reopened with the same symbols
$$ afterwards using analyze_dump.cdb.
$$ Armed on BOTH chances. int 29h is noncontinuable, so the first real one of
$$ these arrived as a second-chance event and walked straight past a first-chance
$$ -c: we got the header line and none of the report, with the process frozen at a
$$ prompt holding everything we had asked for.
sxe -c ".echo ===FAILFAST===; r rcx; .echo ---STACK---; kv; .echo ---ALLTHREADS---; ~*k; .echo ---END---; .dump /ma /o F:/rbo_fabre_proto/d3d9_sw/failfast.dmp" -c2 ".echo ===FAILFAST-2ND===; r rcx; .echo ---STACK---; kv; .echo ---ALLTHREADS---; ~*k; .echo ---END---; .dump /ma /o F:/rbo_fabre_proto/d3d9_sw/failfast.dmp" 0xc0000409

$$ Heap corruption, the other way a bad restore announces itself.
sxe -c ".echo ===HEAPCORRUPT===; r rcx; .echo ---STACK---; kv; .echo ---ALLTHREADS---; ~*k; .echo ---END---; .dump /ma /o F:/rbo_fabre_proto/d3d9_sw/heapcorrupt.dmp" -c2 ".echo ===HEAPCORRUPT-2ND===; r rcx; .echo ---STACK---; kv; .echo ---ALLTHREADS---; ~*k; .echo ---END---; .dump /ma /o F:/rbo_fabre_proto/d3d9_sw/heapcorrupt.dmp" 0xc0000374

$$ Ordinary access violations, second chance only. Mono raises first-chance
$$ exceptions as routine control flow, so acting on those would never finish.
$$ sxd passes the first chance through to the program's own handler and reports
$$ only if nothing handled it, which is the case that actually kills the process.
sxd -c2 ".echo ===AV===; .exr -1; .echo ---STACK---; kv; .echo ---END---" av

g
